'''usager''' (ū′zā-jėr), ''n.'' [< F. ''usager'', < ''usage'', usage: see ''usage''.] '''1.''' One who has the use of anything in trust for another. ''Daniel.''—'''2.''' One of a party which maintained the usages (see phrase under ''usage'') among the English non-jurors and in the Scottish Episcopal Church.

'''usance''' (ū′za̤ns), ''n.'' [< ME. ''usaunce'', < OF. ''usance'', < ''usant'', using: see ''usant''.] '''1†.''' Using; use; employment.

{{smaller block|By this discriminative ''usance'' or sanctification of things sacred the name of God is honoured and sanctified.
{{right|''Joseph Mede'', Diatribe, p. 60.}}
But why do you call this benefit made of our money usury and madness? It is but ''usance'', and husbanding of our stock. {{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 281.}}}}
'''2†.''' Usage; custom.
{{smaller block|<poem>As was her ''usaunce''
To forthren every wight, and doon plesaunce
Of veray bounte and of courtesye.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 1476.}}}}
'''3†.''' Premium paid for the use of money loaned; interest.
{{smaller block|<poem>He lends out money gratis and brings down
The rate of ''usance''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 3. 46.}}}}
'''4.''' The time which is allowed by custom or usage for the payment of bills of exchange drawn on a distant country. {{smaller|The length of the usance varies in different places from fourteen days to six months after the date of the bill, and the bill may be drawn at usance, half usance, double usance, etc. In recent years a four months’ usance has been established for India, China, Japan, etc.}}

'''usant†''' (ū′za̤nt), ''a.'' [< ME. ''usaunt'', < OF. ''usant'', ppr. of ''user'', use: see ''use''.] Using; accustomed.
{{smaller block|<poem>A theef he was of corn and eek of mele,</poem>}}

