'''usaunce†, usaunt†.''' Old spellings of ''usance'', ''usant''.

'''Usbeg,''' ''n.'' See ''Uzbeg''.

'''uscher†,''' ''n.'' An old spelling of ''usher''.

'''Uscock''' (us′kok), ''n.'' [= G. pl. ''Uskoken'', Serbo-Croatian fugitives.] One of the dwellers in Servia and Bosnia who about the beginning of the sixteenth century settled in Dalmatia and neighboring regions, on account of the Turkish invasions.

'''use<sup>1</sup>''' (ūs), ''n.'' [< ME. ''use'', ''uce'', ''us'', < OF. ''us'', ''uz'' = Pr. ''us'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''uso'', < L. ''usus'', use, experience, discipline, skill, habit, custom, < ''uti'', pp. ''usus'', OL. ''oeti'', pp. ''oesus'', use, employ, exercise, perform, enjoy, etc.; cf. Skt. ''ūta'', pp. of √ ''av'', favor. Hence ult. ''use'', ''v.'', ''usage'', ''usual'', ''usurp'', ''usury'', ''utensil'', ''utilize'', ''utility''; ''abuse'', ''peruse''; ''disuse'', ''misuse'', etc.] '''1.''' The act of employing anything, or the state of being employed; employment; application; conversion to a purpose, especially a profitable purpose.
{{smaller block|This word habbeth muchel on ''vs''. {{right|''Ancren Riwle'', p. 16.}}
The fat of the beast that dieth of itself . . . may be used in any other ''use''. {{right|Lev. vii. 24.}}
I know not what ''use'' to put her to.
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iii. 2. 97.}}
<poem>''Sub.'' Why, this is covetise!
''Mam.'' No, I assure you,
I shall employ it all in pious ''uses''.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, ii. 1.}}}}
